jekyll-enough becomes a submodule — and the guard from #233 did not actually work

The swap itself, plus a **correction to #233**: the guard it added tested for the
wrong thing and would have let this land silently broken.

## The swap

    git rm -r jekyll-enough
    git submodule add https://github.com/FCCN-ANTIBODY/jekyll-enough.git jekyll-enough

Same path, so nothing that imports it changes — that was the whole premise and it
held. All eight files went across **byte-identical** (`cmp` on each, plus
`diff -rq` on `fixtures/`), and the new repository carries the thirteen original
commits with their authors intact.

**117 suites before, 118 after.** The same four run out of the submodule;
`dependencies.test.mjs` came with it, which is the family's promise as a test —
a production module may import a sibling and nothing else, and today the four
import zero `node:` builtins between them.

## The correction, and it is the important half

#233 made `scripts/test.mjs` fail on a missing listed directory. **An unhydrated
submodule is not a missing directory.** Git creates the mount point and leaves it
empty, so `readdirSync` does not throw — it returns `[]`. The check caught a rename
and missed the case it was written for.

Measured on this commit, with a real submodule rather than a renamed folder:

    $ git submodule deinit -f jekyll-enough
    $ node scripts/test.mjs
    ...
    113/113 suites passed          ← exit 0, and zero jekyll-enough suites ran

That is precisely the failure #233 was meant to prevent, still happening, one commit
after the PR that claimed to fix it. It was only findable by deinit-ing an actual
mount — which could not be done before the repository existed, so #233 could not
have caught it and this PR is where it was always going to surface.

The test is now **"this listed directory contributed no suites"**, never "this
directory is absent":

    $ git submodule deinit -f jekyll-enough && node scripts/test.mjs
    test: 1 listed directory contributes no suites: jekyll-enough
          An unhydrated submodule looks exactly like this — the directory is there and empty:
            git submodule update --init --recursive
          In CI that is `submodules: recursive` on actions/checkout.
          If a directory was renamed or genuinely has no tests, update the list in this file.
    exit 1

    $ git submodule update --init jekyll-enough && node scripts/test.mjs
    118/118 suites passed          ← exit 0

Every listed directory has suites today — 64, 20, 10, 7, 5, 5, 5, 2 — so the
stricter rule has nothing to false-positive on. A member that legitimately has no
tests does not belong in a list whose only purpose is to find them.

Worth noting the two runners that already got this right, since it says where the
mistake came from: `jekyll-enough`'s own `test.mjs` and its `dependencies.test.mjs`
both test for *zero findings*, because they were written knowing they might be
handed an empty checkout. The one that assumed a filesystem was the one that had
always had the files sitting there.

## Not in scope

`docs/origin.md` is updated where it hedged on the repository existing, and the
order now names "prove the guard on a real mount" as its own step for the next
member. The rest of the family has not moved — `cron-enough` and `node-enough` are
next, and the bottle cluster's served-URL question is still open and still yours.
